# Wall panel: re-mirror the FRAME VIDEO share only (OI-18 exit (b), the Owner,
# 2026-08-03; cadence from Personal\homelab\deploy\storage-map.md §4d).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS AS A SECOND UNIT AT ALL — the decision, stated once:
# the panel's two media sources have DIFFERENT CADENCES, and one unit cannot
# carry both. §4d gives `sync-frame-videos` a ONE-MINUTE accessibility-checked
# timer; `sync-music` has no such cadence and OI-15 deliberately gave the music
# pull boot + resume + on demand and NO timer. Running the pair together every
# minute would re-walk the whole music library over 802.11 from a 2016 laptop's
# radio sixty times an hour, and the first music sync alone is allowed up to an
# hour (wall-sync.service's TimeoutStartSec). So: one script, one code path, two
# units — this one passes `--only frame`.
#
# ONE SCRIPT, NOT TWO. Everything about the mirror, the --delete guards and the
# manifest contract is identical between the flows; only the address, the
# credential, the subdirectory shape and the failure policy differ, and those are
# in wall-sync.sh's flow map. A second script would be a second place for the
# guards to drift out of.
#
# IT IS NOT ENABLED ITSELF — wall-sync-frame.timer is. Nothing should start this
# at boot: wall-sync.service already mirrors both flows on the boot pass, and two
# frame mirrors racing at boot is exactly what the per-flow lock exists to stop
# (it would take the lock, log, and do nothing — correct, but noise).
#
# NORMAL OPERATION INCLUDES DOING NOTHING. Mini-serv is allowed to sleep and is
# never woken for this, so an unreachable share is a SKIP, not a failure, and
# this unit exits 0. What it does NOT do is go quiet: every skip logs the AGE of
# the cached content, and a mount that is REFUSED BY AN AWAKE BOX still fails.
[Unit]
Description=Wall panel: mirror the Mini-serv frame-video share into the local media cache (every minute)
Documentation=file:///opt/wall-panel/stack/autoinstall/wall/README.md
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
After=wall-firstboot.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/wall-sync.sh --only frame
# A BACKSTOP, NOT THE PRIMARY BOUND. wall-sync.sh now carries the frame flow's
# OWN budget and the arithmetic is: probe 4 s + mount 20 s + rsync 14400 s +
# unmount 30 s = 14454 s worst case, so this is set above that. The script
# hitting its own budget produces a NAMED failure ("rsync ... exceeded its
# 14400s budget"); systemd hitting this one produces a SIGTERM and no
# explanation, which is why the script must always get there first.
#
# THE OLD 180 s, AND THE 90 s RSYNC BUDGET IT WAS SIZED FOR, WERE BOTH BUILT ON
# "the frame set is a handful of rendered videos". That was never measured and
# it is false: on 2026-09-16 the share was 29 files totalling 43 GB. Every run
# since the flow shipped was killed mid-mirror, the manifest was never
# regenerated, and the wall said "No frame media configured" -- which reads as a
# missing setting and was actually this number. So the frame flow now carries the
# music flow's arithmetic, for the music flow's reason: the FIRST mirror really
# does take hours -- music measured at about 1 h 54 m for 39.8 GB at 5.5 MiB/s,
# so 43 GB is about 2 h 12 m -- and a budget exists to bound a WEDGED mount, not
# to forbid a large transfer.
#
# THE ONE-MINUTE TIMER DOES NOT STACK BEHIND THIS. wall-sync-frame.timer uses
# OnUnitInactiveSec, which measures from the moment the service goes INACTIVE, so
# a run still going simply postpones the next trigger; there is no queue of fifty
# jobs to grow. That was the real protection all along, not the short timeout.
#
# Every run AFTER the first is an incremental rsync over 29 files and finishes in
# seconds. It is only the first that needs the room.
TimeoutStartSec=15600
# Same politeness as the boot sync: the panel is drawing a UI and playing music
# while this runs, and this one runs sixty times an hour.
IOSchedulingClass=idle
Nice=10
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
